														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4004051">
									

<p>So I actually put off responding to this one for a bit as I collected some thoughts. Your criticism is fair and if the skip doesn't work for you, it doesn't work. That said, I felt I should address a few things:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>'what if [human behaviour which has many well-documented causes] actually had a supernatural explanation' syndrome.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It was not my intention to explain away this phenomenon via this article. If anything, I 'd like to imagine the anomaly happens in tandem with what is observed in real life, which is one of the reasons it's so hard to contain, and why the Foundation is willing to hold people for 6 months to ensure they don't have a false positive when identifying the infected.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Without adding any twists to the formula</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Its true I tried to keep the article as grounded in reality as I could. That was part of the point. But there are some twists thrown in to make this clearly anomalous:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4560 is a memetic infection observed in those who identify as male</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The infection uses the very concept of being male as its means of entry into a victim. This means that the second you start identifying as such you are a new potential target. What would that mean for non-binary people, well, there is a tale idea for sure.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Additionally, at this stage of infection, the individual is capable of generating new vectors of SCP-4560. Such vectors have included:</p>
<ul>
<li>Notes of encouragement.</li>
<li>Thank you emails.</li>
<li>Messages left on voice mail.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>This is where things get particularly sticky for the Foundation. Since vectors of SCP-4560 are so easy to produce and spread, things can quickly get out of hand fast. While cases like Robert Hale's are more easy to spot and quite tragic, I imagine the Foundation has a bigger challenge in dealing with people who recognize their infection and continue to carry on as if everything is fine or even more dangerous, people who don't recognize the change to begin with. A particularly panglossian individual could potentially not recognize they suddenly lost the ability to express negative emotions if they never really did so to begin with. That would make them a particularly virulent creator of SCP-4560 vecotrs and could lead to many, many more people becoming infected, all while the Foundation would have no reliable means of tracking them down aside from following the trail of infected as it were.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Currently, all attempts to cure infected individuals have resulted in failure, though the administration of amnestics has been noted to slow the course of infection.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This final breadcrumb shows that whatever this anomaly is, its significantly tied into memory enough that disrupting the formation of them hinders its progress.</p>
<p>Perhaps this is all too little, too late, but I do feel that there is a lot going on here that allows for a break from the formula, as called it. It's just a little subtle.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><em>Long post collapsed. ~Zyn</em></p>

